WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.952
A city has two bike stations

2
00:00:04.952 --> 00:00:07.216
at the market and at the beach.

3
00:00:07.216 --> 00:00:09.905
Each day, of the bikes at the market

4
00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:12.098
eighty percent stay and twenty

5
00:00:12.169 --> 00:00:14.150
percent end up at the beach.

6
00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.061
Of the bikes at the beach

7
00:00:16.131 --> 00:00:17.971
thirty percent ride to the

8
00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:19.740
market and seventy stay.

9
00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:21.729
That is a matrix P.

10
00:00:21.815 --> 00:00:24.058
Each column says where one

11
00:00:24.144 --> 00:00:25.782
station's bikes go

12
00:00:25.782 --> 00:00:28.456
so each column adds up to one

13
00:00:28.456 --> 00:00:29.922
no bike is lost.

14
00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:32.424
A matrix like that is called

15
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:33.890
a Markov matrix.

16
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.533
All the bikes start at the market.

17
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:41.601
After one day: eighty and twenty percent.

18
00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:44.604
After two: seventy and thirty.

19
00:00:44.604 --> 00:00:47.800
Then sixty-five and thirty-five.

20
00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.051
Where does it settle?

21
00:00:50.051 --> 00:00:51.590
Pause and guess.

22
00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.291
Here is the key picture.

23
00:00:57.291 --> 00:01:01.090
Every split of the bikes lies on one line

24
00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:03.653
the market fraction plus the

25
00:01:03.741 --> 00:01:05.685
beach fraction is one.

26
00:01:05.685 --> 00:01:08.866
The two ends are all at the market

27
00:01:08.866 --> 00:01:10.810
and all at the beach.

28
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.448
Because no bike is lost, P keeps that total.

29
00:01:15.448 --> 00:01:19.736
So one day maps the whole line into itself

30
00:01:19.736 --> 00:01:21.880
and makes it shorter.

31
00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.334
Another day, and it is shorter again.

32
00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:27.934
Day after day the line shrinks

33
00:01:28.018 --> 00:01:29.947
towards a single point

34
00:01:29.947 --> 00:01:33.470
and every start is dragged along with it.

35
00:01:34.231 --> 00:01:37.973
The point that stays is a split that

36
00:01:38.077 --> 00:01:42.234
one more day leaves alone: P v equals v.

37
00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:46.911
That is an eigenvector, with eigenvalue one.

38
00:01:47.261 --> 00:01:51.013
Solve P minus I, times v, equals zero

39
00:01:51.112 --> 00:01:53.778
zero point two v one equals

40
00:01:53.876 --> 00:01:56.147
zero point three v two.

41
00:01:56.147 --> 00:01:59.504
So v is a multiple of three, two

42
00:01:59.504 --> 00:02:02.170
and once the total is one

43
00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:06.021
it is sixty percent and forty percent.

44
00:02:06.371 --> 00:02:07.663
Check the flows

45
00:02:07.744 --> 00:02:10.168
twenty percent of the market's

46
00:02:10.248 --> 00:02:11.541
sixty is twelve

47
00:02:11.622 --> 00:02:14.287
and thirty percent of the beach's

48
00:02:14.368 --> 00:02:16.065
forty is also twelve.

49
00:02:16.065 --> 00:02:18.811
They balance, so nothing changes.

50
00:02:19.391 --> 00:02:21.339
How fast does it settle?

51
00:02:21.339 --> 00:02:24.343
The eigenvalues add up to the trace

52
00:02:24.343 --> 00:02:25.642
one point five

53
00:02:25.642 --> 00:02:28.077
so the other one is one half.

54
00:02:28.077 --> 00:02:30.918
Its eigenvector is one, minus one

55
00:02:30.999 --> 00:02:32.866
it moves bikes from one

56
00:02:32.947 --> 00:02:35.951
station to the other, along the line.

57
00:02:36.301 --> 00:02:38.870
The start is v plus point

58
00:02:38.972 --> 00:02:41.335
four of that direction

59
00:02:41.335 --> 00:02:43.801
and each day halves it.

60
00:02:43.801 --> 00:02:47.500
One day, a second day, a third day

61
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.301
the distance to v halves every time.

62
00:02:51.651 --> 00:02:54.700
A computer finds v with length one

63
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.836
point eight three, point five five.

64
00:02:57.924 --> 00:03:00.014
Divide by its sum to get

65
00:03:00.102 --> 00:03:01.931
a split of the bikes.

66
00:03:02.511 --> 00:03:05.536
Many books write the same chain with rows

67
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.045
adding to one instead of columns.

68
00:03:08.045 --> 00:03:10.111
Does the same v still work?

69
00:03:10.691 --> 00:03:12.876
That matrix is P transpose.

70
00:03:12.876 --> 00:03:14.981
Apply it to sixty, forty

71
00:03:14.981 --> 00:03:17.652
fifty-six and forty-six percent.

72
00:03:17.652 --> 00:03:21.132
It moved, and the total became one hundred

73
00:03:21.213 --> 00:03:24.451
and two percent: off the line of splits.

74
00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:26.674
With rows adding to one

75
00:03:26.674 --> 00:03:29.562
the vector multiplies from the left

76
00:03:29.562 --> 00:03:31.825
and v is a left eigenvector.

77
00:03:31.825 --> 00:03:34.401
Pick one convention and keep it.

78
00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:37.817
Why is one always an eigenvalue?

79
00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:40.476
Add up the rows of P minus I

80
00:03:40.565 --> 00:03:42.958
in every column you get the

81
00:03:43.046 --> 00:03:46.237
column sum minus one, which is zero.

82
00:03:46.237 --> 00:03:48.541
So P minus I is singular.

83
00:03:48.891 --> 00:03:51.251
And no eigenvalue is bigger

84
00:03:51.338 --> 00:03:52.824
than one in size

85
00:03:52.824 --> 00:03:56.321
a Markov matrix spreads each entry out

86
00:03:56.321 --> 00:03:58.331
but never creates any.

87
00:03:58.681 --> 00:04:02.221
When every entry is positive, more is true

88
00:04:02.221 --> 00:04:03.702
one steady state

89
00:04:03.702 --> 00:04:07.407
and every other eigenvalue smaller than one.

90
00:04:07.407 --> 00:04:11.028
So every start ends at v: all at the beach

91
00:04:11.028 --> 00:04:14.321
mostly at the beach, all at the market.

92
00:04:15.839 --> 00:04:17.146
So, to sum up

93
00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:20.879
a Markov matrix maps the line of splits

94
00:04:20.972 --> 00:04:23.492
into itself and shrinks it.

95
00:04:23.586 --> 00:04:26.572
The eigenvector for lambda equal

96
00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:29.559
to one is the point that stays.

97
00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:32.527
The second eigenvalue says how

98
00:04:32.615 --> 00:04:35.058
fast the rest shrinks: here

99
00:04:35.058 --> 00:04:36.629
by half each day.

100
00:04:37.209 --> 00:04:40.562
In part two: chains that never settle

101
00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.914
and the Markov matrix behind a search

102
00:04:44.002 --> 00:04:46.649
engine's ranking of web pages.
